{
  "abstract": "For every squarefree $d>1$ we exhibit one-parameter families of projective K3 surfaces of Picard number 16 with $T_{\\mathbf Q}\\cong U^2\\oplus\\langle-2\\rangle\\oplus\\langle-2d\\rangle$ and real multiplication by $\\mathbf Q(\\sqrt d)$ (explicit self-adjoint operator, certified symbolically in $d$), and deduce from Floccari's theorem (arXiv 2501.02315, Thm 5.11) that the Kuga–Satake correspondence is algebraic and the Hodge conjecture holds for all powers of every member. This covers $\\sqrt{10}$, $\\sqrt{14}$ and the other composite discriminants that Bhattacharjee–Bhattacharya's F3′ section does not reach. We then locate the frontier in transcendental rank six: Floccari's hypothesis is equivalent to Witt index 2 of $T_{\\mathbf Q}$, equivalently $C^+(T)\\cong M_4(K)$, and for $T=\\mathrm{Tr}_{E/\\mathbf Q}W$ to $K=\\mathbf Q(\\sqrt{-\\det T})$ splitting $\\mathrm{Cores}_{E/\\mathbf Q}C_0(W)$. The six-line and Fricke families have index 2, so their RM results are cases of Floccari's. Witt-index-1 RM surfaces exist for every $d$ tested; for them we prove (Hasse norm theorem) that a totally positive twist lands in index 2, reducing HC for $S\\times S$ to the algebraicity of a single non-similarity Hodge morphism $T(S)\\to T(S')$ with $S'$ Floccari-covered. Bearing on HC: unconditional cases by a short deduction from published theorems, and a corrected map of the rank-six real-multiplication frontier.",
